Docker volumes are the preferred mechanism for persisting data generated by and used by Docker containers. Unlike the writable layer
of a container, volumes exist outside the container lifecycle and remain intact even if the container is deleted.

Storage Types
Volumes: Managed by Docker and stored in a part of the host filesystem (/var/lib/docker/volumes/). They are the safest way to persist data.
Bind Mounts: Maps a specific path on the host machine to a path in the container. Highly dependent on the host's file system structure.
tmpfs Mounts: Stored only in the host’s memory. Data is never written to disk and is lost when the container stops.

Why Use Volumes?
Performance: Volumes have higher I/O performance than the container's writable layer.
Sharing: Multiple containers can mount the same volume simultaneously.
Backups: Easier to back up or migrate than data hidden inside a container.
External Storage: Drivers allow you to store data on cloud providers or NFS.

Essential Commands 🛠️
Create: docker volume create <name>
List: docker volume ls
Inspect: docker volume inspect <name> (find the mount point)
Remove: docker volume rm <name>
Prune: docker volume prune (delete all unused volumes)

How to Mount a Volume
Use the -v or --mount flag during docker run:
docker run -d --name my_app -v my_data:/app/data nginx
my_data: The volume name.
/app/data: The path inside the container.

Commands:
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker volume ls    #shows all existing volume list
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker volume create mysql-data
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker inspect mysql-data      #to check the created volume

Now to bind our db container with mysql-data
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$docker ps   

Stop and remove the mysql container

Now attach the newly created mysql-data volume with the mysql container in docker run command
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker run -d --name mysql --network two-tier -v mysql-data:/var/lib/mysql -e 
MY_SQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root MY_SQL_DATABASE=devops mysql

Now restart the flask-app
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker restart <container_id_for_two-tier-flask-app>

Enter some data in the browser
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker volume inspect mysql-data          #you will get the /var/lib/mysql in the Mountpoint section
Now you will see even when you stop and remove mysql container_id, still data will persist

Go inside the container:
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$sudo su
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$cd /var/lib/docker/volumes/mysql-data/_data      
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app/var/lib/docker/volumes/mysql-data/_data$ls      #you will be able to see all the entered text in the browser
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app/var/lib/docker/volumes/mysql-data/_data$cd ..
/var/lib/docker/volumes/mysql-data/_data$docker restart <container_id_for mysql>

You can create a directory also and mount all the data with -v in the docker run command

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker run -d --name mysql --network two-tier -v mysql-data:/var/lib/mysql -e 
MY_SQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root MY_SQL_DATABASE=devops mysql
